WEBVTT

00:00.090 --> 00:01.710
Instructor: So as I've mentioned a couple of times,

00:01.710 --> 00:04.050
not all merges are fast forward merges.

00:04.050 --> 00:06.690
I'm gonna show you an example of that in this video.

00:06.690 --> 00:08.430
So imagine you're working,

00:08.430 --> 00:10.560
I don't know, you're on a team, two people,

00:10.560 --> 00:13.110
and you have your master branch on a project

00:13.110 --> 00:16.440
and then you create your own branch to fix a bug,

00:16.440 --> 00:18.480
or try some experiment out, or whatever it is.

00:18.480 --> 00:20.520
And you're working over there for a few hours

00:20.520 --> 00:21.960
and then you're happy with it,

00:21.960 --> 00:24.780
and you want to merge it back into the master branch.

00:24.780 --> 00:26.760
But then you realize

00:26.760 --> 00:29.370
hey, somebody's already done some work on the master branch,

00:29.370 --> 00:31.440
my teammate, my partner has done work,

00:31.440 --> 00:33.510
since I created my branch.

00:33.510 --> 00:34.920
So there's a commit over here

00:34.920 --> 00:37.590
or maybe 10 commits that I don't have

00:37.590 --> 00:39.570
on my branch on bugfix,

00:39.570 --> 00:40.920
but it is on master.

00:40.920 --> 00:42.360
So when I try and merge,

00:42.360 --> 00:44.400
it's no longer a fast forward.

00:44.400 --> 00:48.030
If I just try and take these two bugfix branch commits,

00:48.030 --> 00:50.700
it's not a matter of just having master catch up.

00:50.700 --> 00:52.620
Now there's new information

00:52.620 --> 00:54.090
that I don't have on bugfix

00:54.090 --> 00:57.240
and there's information on bugfix that master does not have.

00:57.240 --> 00:59.160
So to make this merge happen,

00:59.160 --> 01:00.930
it's not terribly complicated or anything.

01:00.930 --> 01:02.670
We use the exact same commands,

01:02.670 --> 01:06.150
but Git may not be able to do it automatically for us.

01:06.150 --> 01:08.940
It depends on the particulars of our commits

01:08.940 --> 01:10.980
and if there's conflicting information.

01:10.980 --> 01:13.710
For example, what happens if on the master branch,

01:13.710 --> 01:17.040
my partner edited line 59 of a file

01:17.040 --> 01:18.810
and on the bugfix branch,

01:18.810 --> 01:21.930
I edited the same line to be something else?

01:21.930 --> 01:24.780
Which one wins when there's a conflict?

01:24.780 --> 01:26.220
Well, that's something we're going to address

01:26.220 --> 01:27.450
in the next video.

01:27.450 --> 01:29.340
For now, I'm gonna show you an example

01:29.340 --> 01:31.500
where we don't arrive at a conflict

01:31.500 --> 01:32.640
and instead Git is able

01:32.640 --> 01:34.800
to make the merge for us automatically.

01:34.800 --> 01:36.510
So what happens in these scenarios?

01:36.510 --> 01:38.820
When it's not a fast forward merge,

01:38.820 --> 01:41.730
something called a merge commit is generated.

01:41.730 --> 01:44.370
Basically git just makes a commit for us

01:44.370 --> 01:47.880
on the branch that we're merging into, the recipient branch.

01:47.880 --> 01:49.590
It makes a new commit for us

01:49.590 --> 01:52.590
and it will prompt us to include a message.

01:52.590 --> 01:54.570
And one thing I'll just call your attention to

01:54.570 --> 01:58.020
really quickly is that this new merge commit

01:58.020 --> 02:00.120
is the first commit we've seen

02:00.120 --> 02:03.300
that actually has two different parent commits.

02:03.300 --> 02:05.100
Remember every commit has a parent.

02:05.100 --> 02:06.270
So if we take this commit here,

02:06.270 --> 02:08.760
it has one parent, this one has one parent,

02:08.760 --> 02:11.070
this one has this parent

02:11.070 --> 02:13.290
and this parent from bugfix.

02:13.290 --> 02:15.000
So commits can have multiple parents

02:15.000 --> 02:15.870
and in fact they do.

02:15.870 --> 02:17.460
Whenever we make a merge commit,

02:17.460 --> 02:19.320
I just wanna point that out really quickly.

02:19.320 --> 02:21.210
This is something that will come up later on.

02:21.210 --> 02:23.280
Anyway for now, let's try making a merge commit.

02:23.280 --> 02:24.570
So I'll show you this.

02:24.570 --> 02:26.700
However with our RoadtripPlaylist,

02:26.700 --> 02:28.410
as greatest this repo is,

02:28.410 --> 02:29.940
there's quite a few branches going on

02:29.940 --> 02:33.060
and it's kind of difficult to just visually show you

02:33.060 --> 02:34.560
with all this other clutter.

02:34.560 --> 02:37.380
So what I'm gonna do is just duplicate

02:37.380 --> 02:38.700
some of what we've done.

02:38.700 --> 02:41.400
I'm just gonna make a new repo really quickly.

02:41.400 --> 02:42.900
So I'm gonna back out of here.

02:46.110 --> 02:47.460
I'll call it PlaylistTake2.

02:48.420 --> 02:50.340
I'll cd into that.

02:50.340 --> 02:52.650
Make sure I'm not in a repo, I'll initialize,

02:52.650 --> 02:55.830
and then I'm gonna make a single file on my master branch.

02:55.830 --> 02:58.650
I'm gonna call this songs.txt

02:58.650 --> 03:00.690
and I'm gonna open this up in my editor.

03:00.690 --> 03:02.400
Okay, so here's that file.

03:02.400 --> 03:07.400
I'll add a new commit here, git add songs.txt,

03:07.650 --> 03:11.280
git commit, add songs file.

03:11.280 --> 03:14.070
All right, so I'm just gonna quickly add two songs in here.

03:14.070 --> 03:16.563
Okay, I added two songs by ABBA again.

03:17.520 --> 03:19.800
I've opened this repo in GitKraken.

03:19.800 --> 03:21.420
So you can see master branch

03:21.420 --> 03:24.390
has exactly one commit, add songs file.

03:24.390 --> 03:28.020
Now I'll make my commit in here just to show you again.

03:28.020 --> 03:29.460
I can do it visually.

03:29.460 --> 03:32.250
I click Stage for that file instead of git add

03:32.250 --> 03:36.060
and then add to ABBA songs.

03:36.060 --> 03:37.950
Okay, commit changes to one file.

03:37.950 --> 03:39.270
I'm on the master branch.

03:39.270 --> 03:41.670
Great, so I'm now going to branch

03:41.670 --> 03:43.710
off from this master branch

03:43.710 --> 03:46.530
and I'll make a branch called ABBA, sure.

03:46.530 --> 03:49.530
It's going to include more ABBA songs.

03:49.530 --> 03:51.330
So I'll go ahead and do that.

03:51.330 --> 03:53.580
I'll do it from here, git branch ABBA,

03:54.777 --> 03:56.313
and then I can switch to it,

03:57.510 --> 03:59.370
git switch to ABBA.

03:59.370 --> 04:02.160
Okay, so I'll add another couple ABBA songs.

04:02.160 --> 04:04.050
I'll make a commit on this branch.

04:04.050 --> 04:07.830
All right, so I'll do "Mama Mia" by ABBA

04:07.830 --> 04:09.930
and then I'll stage that file

04:09.930 --> 04:14.040
and then add "Mama Mia,"

04:14.040 --> 04:16.260
and I'll do one more commit.

04:16.260 --> 04:17.190
What's another song?

04:17.190 --> 04:22.190
I guess "Dancing Queen," Dancing Queen ABBA,

04:22.350 --> 04:24.210
and I'll do the same thing.

04:24.210 --> 04:26.340
Definitely do not need to do this with a GUI,

04:26.340 --> 04:28.410
but I'm just trying to show it in two different ways,

04:28.410 --> 04:30.273
so add Mama Mia.

04:31.380 --> 04:33.240
Okay, so now we have what?

04:33.240 --> 04:34.860
Two commits on master branch

04:34.860 --> 04:36.600
and two commits in addition

04:36.600 --> 04:38.670
that are only on the ABBA branch.

04:38.670 --> 04:40.140
So if I were to merge right now,

04:40.140 --> 04:41.550
it would just be a fast forward.

04:41.550 --> 04:45.090
If I merged the ABBA changes into master,

04:45.090 --> 04:46.710
master just catches up.

04:46.710 --> 04:49.030
But I'm gonna go back to master

04:50.730 --> 04:52.290
and do some more work.

04:52.290 --> 04:54.480
So this is the same idea

04:54.480 --> 04:57.000
that I showed here where what if I'm working on a branch

04:57.000 --> 04:58.740
and then a coworker does a new commit

04:58.740 --> 05:00.510
on master in the meantime?

05:00.510 --> 05:02.040
So let's do that.

05:02.040 --> 05:05.100
Let's add a completely new file to master.

05:05.100 --> 05:06.450
Remember I said we can run

05:06.450 --> 05:08.430
into conflicts which complicate things.

05:08.430 --> 05:10.170
I'm gonna show that in the next video.

05:10.170 --> 05:12.210
So that's why I'm gonna do it in a separate file.

05:12.210 --> 05:14.040
I'm gonna make a new commit.

05:14.040 --> 05:15.870
Maybe instead of just songs,

05:15.870 --> 05:18.210
I also want a playlist for podcasts.

05:18.210 --> 05:20.970
So I'm gonna make a podcast file

05:20.970 --> 05:24.393
on the master branch, so podcasts.txt.

05:25.726 --> 05:28.050
Okay, so now I have this new file

05:28.050 --> 05:31.260
and I'll make a commit just to commemorate that.

05:31.260 --> 05:33.600
I'll do it this time from the command line.

05:33.600 --> 05:36.600
So git add podcasts.txt,

05:36.600 --> 05:40.950
git commit, -- podcasts file.

05:40.950 --> 05:42.780
So back in GitKraken,

05:42.780 --> 05:45.990
we see now master has this new commit

05:45.990 --> 05:48.690
that ABBA does not know about.

05:48.690 --> 05:51.060
ABBA knows about these four commits,

05:51.060 --> 05:54.600
the two ABBA commits plus the two prior on master,

05:54.600 --> 05:56.490
but now master has a new commit

05:56.490 --> 05:58.830
and so merging is going to be slightly different.

05:58.830 --> 06:00.960
It's not a fast forward as we've discussed.

06:00.960 --> 06:03.990
I am just gonna add one commit to master.

06:03.990 --> 06:06.720
Maybe one more, let's go to the podcast file

06:06.720 --> 06:08.760
and add something to the podcast file.

06:08.760 --> 06:10.740
How about some good podcasts?

06:10.740 --> 06:13.443
I like one called "The Lead," a sports podcast.

06:14.317 --> 06:16.740
"Radiolab" obvious, well, I shouldn't say obviously,

06:16.740 --> 06:18.457
but a lot of people know that one.

06:18.457 --> 06:21.540
"This American Life," we'll just stop there.

06:21.540 --> 06:26.540
Okay, three podcasts, and I'll add the podcast file

06:26.550 --> 06:31.350
and commit, add three podcasts.

06:31.350 --> 06:33.690
Okay, so now on master,

06:33.690 --> 06:36.120
we have two commits that ABBA doesn't know about

06:36.120 --> 06:38.130
and on ABBA, we have two commits

06:38.130 --> 06:39.840
that master doesn't know about.

06:39.840 --> 06:41.340
So I'm gonna do the merge.

06:41.340 --> 06:43.680
Just like before, I'm going to merge

06:43.680 --> 06:46.350
the ABBA branch onto master.

06:46.350 --> 06:47.550
I shouldn't say just like before

06:47.550 --> 06:49.500
because we haven't had an ABBA branch,

06:49.500 --> 06:52.950
but I'm just gonna do the same process where I go to master,

06:52.950 --> 06:53.970
I'll switch to master,

06:53.970 --> 06:57.270
and then I'll merge the ABBA branch into master.

06:57.270 --> 06:58.740
So this is what it looks like right now.

06:58.740 --> 07:01.680
Just keep your eye on this and remember, right?

07:01.680 --> 07:03.570
We split off to make ABBA

07:03.570 --> 07:05.670
and then I kept going on the master branch,

07:05.670 --> 07:07.980
and I added the podcast file.

07:07.980 --> 07:10.560
So now I'm on the master branch, right?

07:10.560 --> 07:13.290
Just double check, and I'm going to merge ABBA.

07:13.290 --> 07:15.570
So git merge ABBA.

07:15.570 --> 07:17.790
Did I capitalize the branch name?

07:17.790 --> 07:19.380
Yes, I did, okay.

07:19.380 --> 07:20.973
There we are, git merge ABBA.

07:22.290 --> 07:24.390
Oh, and take a look at this.

07:24.390 --> 07:25.770
It opened up my editor

07:25.770 --> 07:30.150
because I've set my default text editor in Git to be VS code

07:30.150 --> 07:32.040
and it's waiting for me to close that file.

07:32.040 --> 07:33.630
Just like when I make a commit,

07:33.630 --> 07:37.320
if I don't use the -m flag, it opens up VS code.

07:37.320 --> 07:39.780
And so this tells us we have a new commits.

07:39.780 --> 07:41.190
Remember I talked about this idea

07:41.190 --> 07:43.500
of a merge commit being made for us.

07:43.500 --> 07:45.570
That's exactly what's going on.

07:45.570 --> 07:47.610
Git made a commit for me

07:47.610 --> 07:51.540
and it suggested a message, Merge branch ABBA.

07:51.540 --> 07:55.230
I can add other stuff in here if I want to.

07:55.230 --> 07:56.490
Usually I just leave this alone

07:56.490 --> 07:58.650
'cause you're just merging the branch

07:58.650 --> 08:00.690
and that's pretty self-explanatory,

08:00.690 --> 08:03.120
but we could edit this commit message.

08:03.120 --> 08:05.250
All we need to do is close.

08:05.250 --> 08:08.040
So if you did configure your editor, just close out

08:08.040 --> 08:09.993
and that will make our new commit.

08:10.830 --> 08:14.130
There we are, it says merge made by the recursive strategy.

08:14.130 --> 08:15.630
We'll learn more about that in,

08:15.630 --> 08:17.910
well, you can in an appendix

08:17.910 --> 08:20.220
a bit where I talk about how Git works.

08:20.220 --> 08:21.570
Totally optional though.

08:21.570 --> 08:24.240
Let's take a look over here in GitKraken.

08:24.240 --> 08:25.620
What do we see now?

08:25.620 --> 08:27.480
Well, this line is new.

08:27.480 --> 08:28.770
See that right there?

08:28.770 --> 08:33.770
In a new commit right there that says Merge branch ABBA.

08:34.110 --> 08:38.310
So I can also view this with git log, I'm on master.

08:38.310 --> 08:40.980
We can see I have the original master commits

08:40.980 --> 08:44.280
and then I have the commits from our ABBA branch.

08:44.280 --> 08:45.930
By the way, I screwed up.

08:45.930 --> 08:48.330
You probably saw this before me.

08:48.330 --> 08:49.860
I gave them the same commit message,

08:49.860 --> 08:51.900
add Mama Mia, add Mama Mia,

08:51.900 --> 08:55.020
even though one of them was actually adding Dancing Queen

08:55.020 --> 08:57.330
and the other was adding Mama Mia.

08:57.330 --> 08:59.700
Anyway we have those two

08:59.700 --> 09:01.800
and I have my commits from the master branch,

09:01.800 --> 09:04.260
add podcast file, add three podcasts,

09:04.260 --> 09:07.080
and then I have this merge commit that Git made for me,

09:07.080 --> 09:09.660
and I just left the default message.

09:09.660 --> 09:13.440
So that's an example of a non-fastforward merge

09:13.440 --> 09:16.020
where things were still automatically merged.

09:16.020 --> 09:17.640
I just had to provide a message.

09:17.640 --> 09:19.950
There was no conflicting changes.

09:19.950 --> 09:22.020
There was no problems that arose.

09:22.020 --> 09:25.050
For example, I wasn't changing two things in the same file.

09:25.050 --> 09:26.610
Git was able to figure out

09:26.610 --> 09:29.100
oh, just merge in that new stuff

09:29.100 --> 09:32.910
into the actual songs file, this ABBA stuff,

09:32.910 --> 09:35.040
and keep the podcast changes

09:35.040 --> 09:37.470
that are on the master branch already.

09:37.470 --> 09:39.570
So that's one example of a merge commit.

09:39.570 --> 09:40.500
In the next video,

09:40.500 --> 09:42.720
I'll talk about what happens when there is a conflict

09:42.720 --> 09:43.923
and how we resolve that.
